; `tyCompat` treated a type variable on either side, at any depth, as
; matching anything - no substitution exists, so `Tree a` had to pass
; against `Tree Int`. The consequence was that writing `a` was
; strictly WEAKER than writing `Int`:
;
;   (:: bad (-> Int Int))  (fn (bad x) (+ x 1))
;   (bad (Pt 3 4))            AX3004: expected Int, found Pt   exit 1
;
;   (:: bad (-> a   Int))  (fn (bad x) (+ x 1))
;   (bad (Pt 3 4))            prints 4372529169                exit 0
;
; The struct handle arrived in `x`, `+` added one to a pointer, and the
; program answered an address - while `README.md:1187` listed
; "polymorphic signatures" under **Complete**.
;
; Two shapes, both in the DEFINITION, which is where the wrong answer
; came from:
;
;   bad       performs arithmetic on a value whose type the caller
;             chooses. There is no such operation;
;   promised  declares that it answers the caller's first type and
;             answers the second one instead.
;
; A CALL SITE is checked too, since `pinArg` (2026-08-21): the first
; argument PINS each variable, so `(f 1 (Pt 3 4))` on `(-> a a Int)` is
; AX3004 at the second argument - `expected Int, found Pt` - which is
; `tests/diagnostics/998-placeholder-pinned.ax`'s subject. Before that,
; instantiation minted a placeholder and never solved it. The programs
; that must keep working are `tests/selfhost/972-polymorphic-signature.ax`.
(struct Pt
  (x : Int)
  (y : Int))

(:: bad (-> a Int))

(fn (bad x) (+ x 1))

(:: promised (-> a b a))

(fn (promised x y) y)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (bad (Pt 3 4)))
